// TEST: reject
// BACKENDS: c11, llvm, modest
// EXPECT-ERROR: variable of unsized array type requires an initializer
// EXPECT-ERROR: variable of unsized array type requires an initializer
// EXPECT-ERROR: extern is allowed only for global definitions
//
// A variable of an unsized array type `[]T`, and where its size may come
// from.
//
// The length of an array is part of its type, so `[]T` — a type that
// leaves it out — says nothing about how much storage to reserve.  A
// variable of it therefore needs an initializer to take the size from;
// without one the backend has nothing to write down: the C output would be
// `int32_t a[];`, which clang refuses.
//
// The one variable that may leave `[]T` empty is an `@extern` global: its
// storage is defined outside the module, and the size along with it — see
// `unsized_extern.modest` for that side of the rule.  A local is storage
// this function makes, so the escape hatch does not apply to it, and
// `@extern` on a local is refused outright rather than quietly ignored.
//
// `[]T` as a *parameter* or behind a pointer stays legal — nothing here is
// about the type itself, only about a variable that has to be storage.

include "libc/ctypes64"


// Nothing defines this elsewhere, so the size has to come from right here.
var table: []Int32


func main () -> Int {
	// A local has no other source for a size either.
	var local: []Int32

	// And it cannot borrow one from outside: a local is never external.
	@extern("C")
	var borrowed: []Int32

	return 0
}
